{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1198. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful ruler and a skilled diplomat navigating complex political landscapes in the Levant. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and extensive building projects solidifying Tripoli's position as a major power in the region.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond III was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Sophia of Armenia. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. This succession was not without its challenges. Raymond faced internal opposition from disgruntled vassals and external threats from Muslim forces. His early years focused on consolidating his authority and securing the borders of his county. He skillfully used diplomacy and military force to quell rebellions and maintain control.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant military activity. He engaged in numerous campaigns against the Ayyubid dynasty a rising power in the Middle East. These conflicts involved sieges battles and strategic alliances with other Crusader states. He fought against Saladin and his successors defending Tripoli's independence and trade routes.  He also dealt with incursions from nomadic tribes and opportunistic Muslim rulers seeking to exploit weaknesses in the Crusader defenses. He frequently relied on mercenaries to augment his forces demonstrating a pragmatic approach to warfare.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his adept diplomacy. He fostered alliances with other Crusader states including the Kingdom of Jerusalem the Principality of Antioch and the County of Edessa. These alliances were crucial for maintaining a united front against Muslim aggression. He also engaged in negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking truces and agreements that would ensure the safety of Tripoli's merchants and citizens. His diplomatic efforts were often complex and required careful balancing of interests. He understood that military strength alone was insufficient to secure Tripoli's future.\n\nRaymond III was a patron of the arts and architecture. He oversaw the construction of several important buildings including churches mosques and fortifications.  The Great Mosque of Tripoli was expanded and strengthened during his reign reflecting his commitment to both religious and civic life.  He also supported the development of trade and commerce promoting Tripoli as a major commercial center in the Levant. His reign represents a period of relative stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli a testament to his political acumen and leadership skills.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1198. His death marked the end of a long and eventful reign. He left behind a county that was stronger and more prosperous than when he had inherited it. He is remembered as a capable ruler a skilled diplomat and a valiant warrior. His efforts to maintain Tripoli's independence and prosperity during a turbulent period in Crusader history earned him respect both within and outside the Crusader states. His legacy continued to influence the County of Tripoli for many years after his death shaping its political and cultural landscape.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2584795355796814}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.2702900469303131}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.07248647511005402}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0002783149539027363}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.6464111215318553e-05}, {"token": "